{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "\nThe rapid adoption of code agents in software development necessitates rigorous safety evaluation and robust defense mechanisms. This paper has reviewed recent advances in this critical area, focusing on three key contributions:\n\n- **RedCode** [1] provides a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating code agent safety, addressing both code generation and execution risks.\n\n- **DecodingTrust** [2] offers insights into the trustworthiness challenges of GPT models, with implications for code agent safety.\n\n- **BlueCodeAgent** [3] introduces automated blue teaming approaches for developing defensive strategies in code generation AI systems.\n\nTogether, these contributions highlight the importance of systematic evaluation and defense development for code agents. As these systems continue to advance, the research community must prioritize safety to ensure that the benefits of code agents can be realized without unacceptable risks.\n\nFuture research should address the open challenges identified in this paper, including scalability, evaluation metrics, cross-domain generalization, adversarial robustness, and human-AI collaboration. By advancing our understanding of code agent safety and developing effective defense mechanisms, we can enable the responsible deployment of these powerful tools in software development.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie et al. (8 authors). \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[2] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei et al. (19 authors). \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al. (6 authors). \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n",
